  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nSome lingering showers/drizzle continue across portions of \neastern/southern Minnesota and western Wisconsin early this morning. \nThis precip is being pushed to the south-southeast as a Canadian \nsurface high builds in to the northwest. This has led to an uptick \nin northwesterly winds, with gusts of 20-30 MPH across the eastern \nDakotas and Minnesota. Winds will trend down through late morning as \nthe pressure gradient relaxes. This feature will continue to help \nget the last of the precip out of here (finally!), but clouds will \nlikely be more stubborn. A new bank of stratus is diving south \nacross northwestern Minnesota and eastern North Dakota. Eventually \ndrier air will filter in and we should start to see breaks in the \nclouds late tonight into Friday. Northwest flow will make today and \nFriday fairly chilly with highs in the 40s. Saturday will start off \nin the 20s area wide, but should recover into the upper 40s to mid \n50s during the afternoon. Sunday will be a few degrees warmer with \nlow 60s to start off the work week. These warmer temperatures will \nbe accompanied by dry conditions through the remainder of the \nperiod.",
